WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.836
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy- and Golden Globe-winning

00:00:03.836 --> 00:00:05.871
actor who currently stars as
Logan Roy in "Succession."

00:00:05.871 --> 00:00:08.774
The Season 3 finale
airs this Sunday at 9:00 p.m.

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:11.945
on HBO and is available
to stream on HBO Max.

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.614
Please welcome to the show
Brian Cox!

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:16.950
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.795
-Thank you.

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:33.198
-Welcome to the show, Brian.
-Thank you.

00:00:33.198 --> 00:00:34.433
-I've been a fan of yours

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:37.336
for a long time
and love you in "Succession."

00:00:37.336 --> 00:00:38.404
I've been lucky enough to have

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:39.539
some of your castmates
on the show.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:42.475
And I ask them --
I ask them on camera,

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:43.809
"What is Brian like
in real life?"

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:45.912
And they've said you're a
sweetheart and a Teddy bear,

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:49.215
but in both cases, like,
maybe they didn't quite mean it.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:53.753
-It's in their interests
not to mean it.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.554
-Yeah.

00:00:55.554 --> 00:00:58.758
It's such a fantastic --
For anyone who is behind enough

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.526
not to be watching "Succession,"

00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:04.664
you play the patriarch of
this, you know, media family

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:09.068
and, sort of at their core,
really awful people.

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:12.305
But I read that you say
it was very important to you,

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:15.341
when you took the part,
that the character Logan Roy,

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.376
you asked
the creator of the show,

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:17.377
"Does he love his children?"

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:18.745
-Yeah.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.145
-Why was that an important
question for you to ask him?

00:01:20.145 --> 00:01:23.249
-Because he seems to be
a man who is loveless,

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:27.153
and it's very important that
people know that --

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:28.754
Well, actually,
it's not important

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:30.756
that the audience knows at all,
but it's very important

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:34.127
that I knew that I did,
indeed, love my children.

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:36.329
And, of course,
once you have that in mind

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:39.032
and that as part of you,
that enables you to do

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:41.467
all kinds of things
within the role.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.203
And sometimes, you obfuscate,

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:45.304
you don't tell the truth
about your situation,

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:46.772
and he does that a lot.

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:50.743
He keeps people at a distance,
almost deliberately, you know?

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:54.780
And it's also, he's got his own
crap, as well, you know.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.082
-It works, the show.

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:57.283
I think one of the reasons
people are drawn to it,

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.052
it works incredibly well
as a drama.

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:01.487
It's also deeply funny.
-Very funny.

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:04.757
-You have some
incredible lines on the show.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:09.328
Are they as much fun to say as
they are to hear when watching?

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:10.930
-Yeah, I mean, they are.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.399
Especially when they give them
to you at the last minute.

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:14.767
-Okay. So they're not in
the script, per se.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.135
-They're not in the script.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:18.671
And then, suddenly, he'll say,
"Why don't you say that

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:20.172
your 'unh' tastes like
cream cheese

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:21.207
when it 'unh-unhs'?"

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:23.342
And I go, "Oh, okay, fine."

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:24.643
So, I walk on and I do,

00:02:24.643 --> 00:02:26.712
"Your 'unh' is the 'unh'
when it 'unh-unhs.'"

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:30.282
And then I've said it and it's
done and it's out there.

00:02:30.282 --> 00:02:32.985
-If you have a catchphrase,
I'm gonna show you

00:02:32.985 --> 00:02:34.653
that we don't have to do what
you've been doing.

00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:36.188
If you have a catchphrase,
it's "[Bleep] off."

00:02:36.188 --> 00:02:37.991
-"[Bleep] off."
-Yeah.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.192
And you actually wore the --

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:44.296
It was very appropriately
on a mask when you --

00:02:44.296 --> 00:02:47.166
Is that something that now --
Is the character popular enough

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:49.335
that fans want you to say
it to them?

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:50.769
-Oh, all the time.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:54.840
I mean, it's one of the
great things to say to somebody,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.942
'cause, you know,
they'll come up to you and say,

00:02:56.942 --> 00:02:59.011
"Uh, could you say
'[Bleep] off'?"

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:01.214
And you go, "[Bleep] off."

00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:02.448
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:03.783
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:05.585
-That's really nice.

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:10.056
-You know, and, so,
my best story about that --

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:13.892
and this is absolutely true --
so, I'm in L.A.,

00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:15.528
and it was at the time of
the Golden Globes.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:18.497
And Rosanna Arquette,
who's a lovely lady,

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:20.799
invited me --
We did a movie together.

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:24.470
She invites me to this afternoon
with Ronan Farrow.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.872
And he's reading from his book,

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:29.375
and it's very intense
and very incredible,

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.844
what he's writing about.

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:36.982
And all these Hollywood women
are completely attentive to him,

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:39.886
you know, as he's going through
chapter and verse of his book.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:42.120
And, you know,
it's amazing work.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.457
And, so, I arrive late,
so I'm standing at the back.

00:03:45.457 --> 00:03:49.028
And I'm looking at this and
thinking, "Wow, this is intense.

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.063
This is really intense,
all this stuff."

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:52.298
And then he finishes,

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:53.999
and they give him
a round of applause.

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:56.335
And then they turn
'round and they see me

00:03:56.335 --> 00:03:59.038
and they all come at me
and they take their device out

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:03.409
and they say, "Can you tell me
to [bleep] off?"

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:11.718
And you go,
"This is a MeToo meeting.

00:04:13.352 --> 00:04:16.923
And I am a white dinosaur.

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:19.325
And you're asking this
white dinosaur

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:24.930
at this MeToo meeting
to tell you to [bleep] off?

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.733
Something doesn't seem right."

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:28.000
-Right. Sounds like a trap.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.002
-Oh, it does.

00:04:30.002 --> 00:04:33.205
But, also, you know --
And that's human nature.

00:04:33.205 --> 00:04:35.174
-Yeah.
People like what they like.

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:36.242
It doesn't matter the context.

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:39.311
-Human beings are so confused.

00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:41.180
-When I watch Logan Roy,

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.581
you know,
when I saw you in the part,

00:04:42.581 --> 00:04:47.887
I thought, the first time I ever
saw you on film was "Manhunter,"

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:49.421
which people -- is a great film.

00:04:49.421 --> 00:04:50.689
-You're not old enough, surely.

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:52.224
-No, I worked in a video store,
so I got to --

00:04:52.224 --> 00:04:53.293
-Oh, I see.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:55.160
I got to watch --
I got to rent videos.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.563
And "Manhunter,"
for people that don't know,

00:04:57.563 --> 00:05:00.065
you played Hannibal Lecktor for
the first time on film,

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:03.303
in this fantastic
Michael Mann movie.

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:05.304
And I would imagine --
Is it dissimilar?

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:07.273
'Cause you've obviously played
a lot of villains

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:10.742
throughout your career --
some fictional, some real-life.

00:05:10.742 --> 00:05:12.545
Do you always approach it the
same way as Logan Roy?

00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:14.880
Do you have to find some part of
them to be empathetic towards?

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.315
-Well, you have to find --

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:21.587
The rule number one is, when you
play a role, you don't judge.

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:24.490
You must never judge,
because that guides you

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:28.060
into all kinds of territory
which is meaningless.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.028
So you don't judge
who you're playing.

00:05:30.028 --> 00:05:33.265
You play it from
his point of view,

00:05:33.265 --> 00:05:36.002
and however confused
that point of view may be.

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:38.037
And you have to find
a way into that,

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:41.207
and that's, to me, part of
the craft of being an actor.

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:43.842
You have to actually make
it real, make it true.

00:05:43.842 --> 00:05:47.780
I mean, if you're playing
Hermann Goering, for example,

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.448
you know, who is regarded
as probably one of

00:05:49.448 --> 00:05:52.151
the most vile people of
the 20th century,

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:55.054
you know that his background
was very complicated,

00:05:55.054 --> 00:06:00.426
because he was a major flier
in the German air force.

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:02.861
He abandoned his planes
in Switzerland

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:05.564
because he was disgusted by
the Treaty of Versailles.

00:06:05.564 --> 00:06:07.333
He then went
and had a nervous breakdown

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:09.235
and lived in Sweden
for some time.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:14.106
Then he came back and he noticed
this young painter from Vienna

00:06:14.106 --> 00:06:17.142
was stirring everything up,
and he put his whole thing

00:06:17.142 --> 00:06:22.214
towards, you know,
the future of Adolf Hitler.

00:06:22.214 --> 00:06:24.049
So you realize
that there's a man

00:06:24.049 --> 00:06:25.951
who came from a certain
point of view

00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:28.921
and arrived at a pretty hideous
point of view, you know.

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:31.957
And I think that's life.
That's what happens.

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:34.260
-I'm reading your book,
which is fantastic.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:35.561
We were talking
about it backstage.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.329
And you said something about

00:06:37.329 --> 00:06:40.032
being an actor
which stuck with me.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.800
You said,
"Every day is a school day."

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.335
Is that something that --

00:06:43.335 --> 00:06:44.770
Obviously, you've been doing
this for a long time.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.073
You know, started onstage,
been doing film and television

00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:49.275
now for a great many years.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:51.143
Do you feel like you're learning
something new every day?

00:06:51.143 --> 00:06:53.479
-All the time.
You never stop learning.

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:56.449
-And are you learning it
from your own trial and error

00:06:56.449 --> 00:06:58.150
or from the people
you work with?

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.354
-I mean, it's basically from
one's own trial and error,

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:04.423
but you also learn from the
struggles of others, as well,

00:07:04.423 --> 00:07:05.624
that you see around you.

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:07.626
And you see,
"Why is there a problem there?",

00:07:07.626 --> 00:07:10.929
and then you examine their
relationship to that problem

00:07:10.929 --> 00:07:14.300
and how they solved
that problem, and that's great.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.301
I mean, that's a wonderful thing
to be part of.

00:07:16.301 --> 00:07:17.903
You know, 'cause they're all --

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:20.372
You know, in my book,

00:07:20.372 --> 00:07:23.742
I was accused of disrespecting
certain actors recently.

00:07:23.742 --> 00:07:25.645
And I don't disrespect anybody

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:27.212
that's involved
in this profession,

00:07:27.212 --> 00:07:30.816
because it's too hard
a profession to disrespect.

00:07:30.816 --> 00:07:33.953
I do have reservations about
certain talent,

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:36.288
and I'll just say, "Well, I'm
not sure that guy or that gal

00:07:36.288 --> 00:07:37.756
is quite this or that,"

00:07:37.756 --> 00:07:39.525
you know, but that's
the way it is.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.726
But I certainly
would not disrespect,

00:07:41.726 --> 00:07:43.796
because it's a tough job.

00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:46.565
But it's a joyous job
at the same time.

00:07:46.565 --> 00:07:49.368
-I would imagine, too, you
end up performing with people

00:07:49.368 --> 00:07:50.903
who have very
different approaches.

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:52.471
There was just a
fascinating profile about

00:07:52.471 --> 00:07:54.239
the wonderful actor
Jeremy Strong,

00:07:54.239 --> 00:07:56.342
who's in your show,
and it strikes me

00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:58.578
that his approach to it
is very different

00:07:58.578 --> 00:08:00.412
from maybe the fellow castmates.

00:08:00.412 --> 00:08:01.447
Is that --
-It certainly is.

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:02.681
I mean, it certainly is.

00:08:02.681 --> 00:08:05.551
I mean, the thing about
Jeremy's approach is,

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:09.422
it works in terms of
what comes out the other end.

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:11.323
My problem with --
And it's not a problem.

00:08:11.323 --> 00:08:13.759
I don't have a problem with
Jeremy, 'cause he's delightful.

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:16.562
I mean, he's one of the best
fathers I've ever come across.

00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:19.598
He has these three little girls,
and he's an extraordinary dad.

00:08:19.598 --> 00:08:23.302
I mean, he really is.
He's a pretty unique individual.

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:26.705
But he does get obsessed
with the work,

00:08:26.705 --> 00:08:30.609
and I worry about
what it does to him.

00:08:30.609 --> 00:08:33.612
Because if you can't
separate yourself --

00:08:33.612 --> 00:08:35.081
You know,
'cause you're dealing with

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:36.248
all this material every day.

00:08:36.248 --> 00:08:37.316
You can't live in it.

00:08:37.316 --> 00:08:39.184
You know, eventually,
you get worn out.

00:08:39.184 --> 00:08:42.254
Like, to me, Dan Day-Lewis
got worn out at 55.

00:08:42.254 --> 00:08:43.589
He decided to retire

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:45.824
'cause he couldn't go on
doing that every day.

00:08:45.824 --> 00:08:47.626
You know, it was too consuming.

00:08:47.626 --> 00:08:49.094
And I do worry about it.

00:08:49.094 --> 00:08:51.764
I mean, but the result --

00:08:51.764 --> 00:08:53.666
Whatever one says about Jeremy,
the result

00:08:53.666 --> 00:08:56.769
is always extraordinary
and excellent.

00:08:56.769 --> 00:08:59.138
-Can I --
Yeah, please, for Jeremy.

00:08:59.138 --> 00:09:01.173
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:04.009
Can I take a moment?

00:09:04.009 --> 00:09:05.878
I have a very good
Kendall Roy impression

00:09:05.878 --> 00:09:06.879
I'd like to do for you.

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:08.547
-Yeah.
-Alright.

00:09:08.547 --> 00:09:12.418
"Uh...Dad..."

00:09:12.418 --> 00:09:14.487
[ Laughter ]

00:09:14.487 --> 00:09:16.154
Pretty good, right?
-It's that face.

00:09:16.154 --> 00:09:17.423
-Yeah.
-He has that face.

00:09:17.423 --> 00:09:18.858
-I have a face --

00:09:18.858 --> 00:09:20.226
I have a good starting-point
face for Kendall.

00:09:20.226 --> 00:09:23.829
-Yeah, you have a very good
Kendall Roy face.

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:27.332
-The thing I had to -- Like, my
whole day was working on that.

00:09:27.332 --> 00:09:28.734
-Well, you did it very well.
-Thank you.

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:31.704
-And it was so incredibly brief.

00:09:31.704 --> 00:09:33.272
-Thank you. Well, that's...

00:09:33.272 --> 00:09:36.174
You know, I don't know what
you heard from

00:09:36.174 --> 00:09:37.910
your acting teachers,
but mine always said --

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.446
After they watched me,
they said, "Maybe shorter?"

00:09:42.448 --> 00:09:43.982
-So you clearly
learned the lesson.

00:09:43.982 --> 00:09:47.085
-Just keep it shorter.
They were like, "Try brief."

00:09:47.085 --> 00:09:48.787
I mean, you talk -- You know,
you've always played

00:09:48.787 --> 00:09:52.457
a lot of Shakespeare onstage,
and that strikes me

00:09:52.457 --> 00:09:56.562
as very a grueling
part of your early career --

00:09:56.562 --> 00:09:57.863
And even later career, like,

00:09:57.863 --> 00:09:59.632
doing stage work like that.

00:09:59.632 --> 00:10:02.434
Does it -- Like, when you
find yourself doing television,

00:10:02.434 --> 00:10:04.870
does it feel like a relief
compared to those days?

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.640
-No, I mean, television has a
different set of problems.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.709
I mean, you have to sustain,
for one thing,

00:10:10.709 --> 00:10:14.180
and you have these times --
I mean, I'm very lucky,

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:17.383
because I'm blessed
with probably the best crew

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:19.752
that I've ever worked with,
and they're fast,

00:10:19.752 --> 00:10:21.920
and you never notice
the hanging about,

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.989
whereas, a lot of films,
you're waiting for the lighting

00:10:23.989 --> 00:10:26.458
to get done and everything,
so you have to develop

00:10:26.458 --> 00:10:29.228
a great deal of
patience in the working of it.

00:10:29.228 --> 00:10:33.065
But, I mean, theater's just --
It's a different animal.

00:10:33.065 --> 00:10:35.601
And, as a different animal,
it has a different set of

00:10:35.601 --> 00:10:38.904
fears and gratitudes
at the same time.

00:10:38.904 --> 00:10:41.306
-Well, it's wonderful to
see you in any medium,

00:10:41.306 --> 00:10:42.842
and I really appreciate you
making time for us today.

00:10:42.842 --> 00:10:44.076
-Not at all.
It's lovely to be here.

00:10:44.076 --> 00:10:45.410
-Thank you so much.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:46.545
-Brian Cox, everybody.

00:10:46.545 --> 00:10:48.680
The Season 3 finale
of "Succession"

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:49.882
airs Sunday at 9:00 p.m.

00:10:49.882 --> 00:10:52.951
on HBO and is available to
stream on HBO Max.

00:10:52.951 --> 00:10:54.120
And do check out his book,
as well.

